# Getting your application onto OpenShift: a guide for developers

| | |
|---|---|
| **For** | developers on an application team who need access to their team's OpenShift namespaces |
| **You need** | your application's mnemonic, seven directory groups, and namespaces carrying two labels |
| **Checklist** | [ONBOARDING_CHECKLIST.md](ONBOARDING_CHECKLIST.md): the same steps as tick boxes |

Access to OpenShift is never given to you by name. It comes from seven directory (OUD/LDAP) groups that belong to
your application's **mnemonic**, and from two labels on each of your namespaces that name that mnemonic. Get the
groups in place first, then ask for your namespaces. Every example below uses the mnemonic `pay`; use your own.

## 1. What you get

Your application has namespaces in four environments: `rnd` (development), `qa`, `uat` and `prod` (production).
For each environment there is an **audit** group, which lets its members see everything (OpenShift's `view` role), and
for every environment except `prod` there is a **developer** group, which lets its members deploy and change things
(OpenShift's `edit` role).

| Group | Its members can | In |
|---|---|---|
| `app-ocp-rbac-pay-rnd-ns-audit` | view | `rnd` |
| `app-ocp-rbac-pay-qa-ns-audit` | view | `qa` |
| `app-ocp-rbac-pay-uat-ns-audit` | view | `uat` |
| `app-ocp-rbac-pay-prod-ns-audit` | view | `prod` |
| `app-ocp-rbac-pay-rnd-ns-developer` | edit | `rnd` |
| `app-ocp-rbac-pay-qa-ns-developer` | edit | `qa` |
| `app-ocp-rbac-pay-uat-ns-developer` | edit | `uat` |

**Nobody has edit in `prod`.** In production you can look, not change: there is no `prod` developer group.

## 2. Before you start

- **Your mnemonic.** Three lowercase letters assigned to your application, such as `pay`. If you do not know it, ask
  the mnemonic owner.
- **The mnemonic owner.** The person accountable for your application's access. They approve who joins each group,
  and they remove people who no longer need access.
- **The one rule that makes it work.** The mnemonic is spelled exactly the same in all seven group names and in the
  `@LABEL_DOMAIN@/mnemonic` label on every one of your namespaces. If the two differ by a single letter, the namespace
  still looks correctly set up, and it gives nobody any access. Nothing reports the mismatch.

## 3. Step 1: the seven groups

Ask for the seven groups through your organisation's directory (OUD) request process. Paste the names exactly; only
the mnemonic changes:

```text
app-ocp-rbac-pay-rnd-ns-audit
app-ocp-rbac-pay-qa-ns-audit
app-ocp-rbac-pay-uat-ns-audit
app-ocp-rbac-pay-prod-ns-audit
app-ocp-rbac-pay-rnd-ns-developer
app-ocp-rbac-pay-qa-ns-developer
app-ocp-rbac-pay-uat-ns-developer
```

Say in the request that:

- the groups are created alongside the other `app-ocp-rbac-` groups, as the same kind of group (`groupOfNames`), so
  that OpenShift picks them up;
- members are added by their full user entry (DN), because a member written any other way is ignored without an
  error;
- the mnemonic owner approves the members of every group.

Put each person only in the groups they need. A developer is usually in the developer groups for `rnd`, `qa` and
`uat`, and in the audit group of any environment they must be able to look at; `app-ocp-rbac-pay-prod-ns-audit` is
for the people who need to see production.

## 4. Step 2: confirm the groups exist before you ask for namespaces

1. **In the directory:** search for `app-ocp-rbac-pay-`. All seven groups are there, spelled as above, and each
   group you need has its members.
2. **In OpenShift:** the groups reach OpenShift on the group sync's next scheduled run. Then open the OCP Access
   Tracking Dashboard: under **Home**, **Your groups** lists the groups you are in.

Why first: a namespace created before its groups exist is set up to give access to groups that are not there yet.
Nothing breaks, nothing works, and nothing tells you. When the groups arrive, access starts by itself, but you will
have spent that time wondering why you cannot get in.

## 5. Step 3: ask for your namespaces

Every namespace you ask for belongs to one environment and carries both labels:

```yaml
metadata:
  labels:
    @LABEL_DOMAIN@/mnemonic: pay          # your mnemonic, exactly as in the group names
    @LABEL_DOMAIN@/app-environment: rnd   # rnd, qa, uat or prod
```

For example:

| Namespace | `@LABEL_DOMAIN@/mnemonic` | `@LABEL_DOMAIN@/app-environment` |
|---|---|---|
| `pay-rnd` | `pay` | `rnd` |
| `pay-qa` | `pay` | `qa` |
| `pay-uat` | `pay` | `uat` |
| `pay-prod` | `pay` | `prod` |

The environment value is exactly `rnd`, `qa`, `uat` or `prod`, in lowercase. Any other value, such as `dev`,
`production` or `Prod`, gives the namespace no access at all. Put both labels in your namespace request, and access
follows from them: you never ask for access to a namespace separately.

## 6. Step 4: check your access

In the **OCP Access Tracking Dashboard**:

- **Home** lists the namespaces you can reach, each "via" the group that gives you access, and **Your groups** lists
  your groups.
- Open a namespace: its page shows the `mnemonic` and `app-environment` labels it carries and, under **Who reaches it,
  and through which group**, the groups with access and their role.

With `oc`, logged in as yourself (these questions are answered for your own account):

```bash
oc auth can-i create deployments -n pay-rnd   # yes, if you are in app-ocp-rbac-pay-rnd-ns-developer
oc auth can-i get pods -n pay-prod            # yes, if you are in app-ocp-rbac-pay-prod-ns-audit
oc auth can-i create deployments -n pay-prod  # no, for everyone
```

## 7. Living with it

- **Joining and leaving.** People are added to and removed from the groups only by the mnemonic owner, in the
  directory. When someone is removed, the group sync takes them out of the OpenShift group on its next run, and the
  dashboard shows the change after that. Until the next run, their access still works; that is expected.
- **A new namespace later.** Ask for it with the same two labels; it gets the same access as your other namespaces in
  that environment.
- **Never ask for access by name.** Access granted to one person directly, instead of through a group, stays when
  that person leaves the team, and access reviews report it as a finding. Ask the mnemonic owner to add you to the
  right group instead.
- **Production stays view-only.** Nobody, including the mnemonic owner, gets edit in `prod`.
- **The quarterly access review.** Each quarter the mnemonic owner reviews the members of the seven groups and
  confirms or removes each one.

## 8. When something is wrong

| What you see | The likely reason | What to do |
|---|---|---|
| I cannot log in to OpenShift at all | Logging in is allowed by the cluster's login group, which is separate from these seven groups | Ask for OpenShift login access through your directory request process |
| I can log in, but my namespace is not listed | You are not in that environment's group yet, or the group has not synced yet | Check **Your groups** on the dashboard's Home; ask the mnemonic owner to add you; check again after the next sync |
| I can see the namespace but cannot deploy | You are only in its audit group, or it is a `prod` namespace | Outside `prod`, ask the mnemonic owner to add you to that environment's developer group |
| I cannot change anything in `prod` | Expected: nobody has edit in `prod` | Nothing; production is view-only |
| Nobody on the team has access to a new namespace | Its labels are wrong: the mnemonic is misspelled, or the environment is not exactly `rnd`, `qa`, `uat` or `prod` | Ask for the namespace's labels to be corrected (section 5) |
| Someone left the team and still has access | They were removed in the directory, and the group sync has not run since | Check again after the next sync |
